,"alignX":54,"alignY":932.5,"disabled":false,"content":{"k":"define","target":{"k":"var","name":"k"},"value":{"k":"range","first":{"k":"real","v":0},"second":null,"last":{"k":"real","v":100}},"global":false,"stored":null,"unit":null,"shown":false}},{"id":"25","left":306,"top":912.5,"alignX":306,"alignY":932.5,"disabled":false,"content":{"k":"define","target":{"k":"index","name":"f","index":[{"k":"id","name":"k"}]},"value":{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"real","v":10},{"k":"id","name":"Hz"}]},{"k":"op","op":"pow","args":[{"k":"real","v":10},{"k":"op","op":"div","args":[{"k":"id","name":"k"},{"k":"real","v":20}]}]}]},"global":false,"stored":null,"unit":null,"shown":false}},{"id":"26","left":54,"top":958.5,"alignX":54,"alignY":958.5,"disabled":false,"content":{"k":"plot","x":{"k":"op","op":"indexer","args":[{"k":"id","name":"f"},{"k":"id","name":"k"}]},"ys":[{"k":"op","op":"div","args":[{"k":"call","fn":{"k":"id","name":"Re"},"args":[{"k":"call","fn":{"k":"id","name":"Z"},"args":[{"k":"op","op":"indexer","args":[{"k":"id","name":"f"},{"k":"id","name":"k"}]}]}]},{"k":"id","name":"R.dc"}]}],"markers":false,"width":280,"height":160,"xAxis":{"min":null,"max":null,"log":true,"unit":{"k":"id","name":"Hz"}},"yAxis":{"min":null,"max":null,"log":true},"traces":[{"color":"#2563eb","weight":2}]}}],"texts":[{"id":"1","left":54,"top":72,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"The skin effect in a wire. Direct current spreads evenly across a conductor, but alternating current crowds towards its surface, so a thick wire carries AC as if it were a thinner tube. Inside a round wire the current density follows the Bessel function J0 of a complex argument."},{"id":"2","left":54,"top":120.5,"width":440,"height":13.5,"text":"A copper bar 10 mm across, and the depth at which a field dies away by 1/e at each frequency."},{"id":"9","left":54,"top":234,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"The field in the wire obeys a Bessel equation whose wavenumber is complex, κ = (1 − i)/δ: it decays and turns in phase at the same rate. The current density at radius r, as a share of the density at the surface:"},{"id":"15","left":364,"top":396.5,"width":184,"height":60,"text":"Blue 60 Hz: nearly even. Orange 1 kHz: the centre carries 40% of the surface density. Red 10 kHz: the current lives in the outer millimetre or so."},{"id":"16","left":54,"top":570.5,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"The resistance. The wire's internal impedance per metre comes from the same functions; its real part is the resistance, compared here with the DC value."},{"id":"23","left":54,"top":864,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"At 60 Hz the bar costs a quarter of a percent more than at DC; at 1 kHz, 45% more; at 10 kHz, four times as much. At high frequency the ratio approaches a/(2δ) + ¼, the resistance of a tube one skin depth thick. Over five decades:"},{"id":"27","left":364,"top":988.5,"width":184,"height":60,"text":"Flat at 1 while the skin depth is larger than the wire, then rising as √f once it is smaller. Try a = 1 mm: the knee moves up by a factor of 25."}],"opaque":[],"origin":0,"tol":0.001,"exactCompare":true,"zeroOverZero":false,"otherRegions":{},"componentOutputs":[]}}